"Shows association at each lag after accounting for intervening lags to support time-series model diagnosis.\n\nUse when: A stationary or suitably transformed series needs autoregressive structure inspection. Direct lag associations must be separated from indirect intermediate-lag effects.\n\nAvoid when: The sequence is irregular, too short, or strongly nonstationary without adjustment.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes lag. Stem height encodes partial autocorrelation conditional on shorter lags.\n\nBuild: Choose the analysis series, maximum lag, and estimation method. Estimate partial autocorrelations with a documented algorithm. Draw zero-based stems and label any approximate reference bounds and lag units.\n\nCheck: Check the estimation method is appropriate for the sample size. Verify lag count does not exhaust available observations. Inspect ACF and the original series before suggesting a model order.\n\nPitfalls: A cutoff pattern is a diagnostic clue, not automatic proof of a specific model. Different PACF estimators can disagree at high lags; avoid overinterpreting unstable tail stems.\n\nExample: Inspect synthetic detrended weekly demand for direct lag-one and lag-two effects using a named PACF estimator.\n\nAlternatives: Autocorrelation plot, Residual sequence plot, Lag plot."
